# Layer-efficiency thresholds for the targets whose waste is a shadowed base layer.
#
# Not a relaxation of .dive-ci, which stays at 0.95, 20MB and 0.10 and still governs php cli, python
# builder and python cli. This file exists because dive counts a file replaced in a later layer as
# waste, and these targets replace files they inherited rather than files they added:
#
#   - openssl. An upstream base ships whatever version was current when it was tagged, and it ages in
#     place. Upgrading it inside the image is the only remediation while no newer base tag exists, and
#     the upgrade leaves the original 5 MB of libcrypto3 behind in the base layer. Every target built
#     on a base this repository upgrades carries that shadow, directly or through its parent stage.
#   - libuuid, in the Python family only. Same shape as openssl and the same remediation, added after
#     a scan reported seven HIGH findings against the copy the base carried. It is 29 KiB, so its own
#     shadow is negligible next to openssl's, and it is listed here because it is the second package
#     upgraded in place rather than because it moves a threshold.
#   - pip, in the Python runtime only. It uninstalls the installer on purpose, which shadows the
#     interpreter's own tree. That waste predates this file, and the 2026-08-11 calibration recorded
#     in .dive-ci covered the runtime with pip already gone and met the strict floors then, so the
#     openssl shadow is what carried it past them rather than pip.
#
# Neither is reachable by pruning a RUN, because neither lives in a RUN this repository authors. The
# targets listed in the Makefile are the ones where the shadow is large next to what the target itself
# adds. The three not listed carry the same shadow but add enough of their own bytes to meet the
# strict floors anyway, so they stay on .dive-ci. One of them failing later is a signal to look for a
# real regression first, not to move it here.
#
# Measured 2026-08-29 at build unit 1.0.3, with the floors set just past the worst of the five:
#
#   php runtime        93.1% efficient, 14.1% user waste, 15.1 MB wasted
#   php development    93.7% efficient, 12.5% user waste, 15.3 MB wasted
#   php builder        93.6% efficient, 12.8% user waste, 15.1 MB wasted
#   python runtime     88.9% efficient, 28.2% user waste, 12.9 MB wasted
#   python development 93.4% efficient, 14.1% user waste, 24.4 MB wasted
#
# An unpruned build dependency or a forgotten cache in any of them still fails here.
#
# Retires when the upgrade lines do, and not when a base happens to be current. Measured again on
# 2026-09-19 at build unit 1.0.4, with both bases shipping the openssl the v3.24 index served, so
# every upgrade resolved to a no-op and nothing was shadowed at all:
#
#   php runtime        99.2% efficient,  1.1% user waste,  1.1 MB wasted
#   php development    99.1% efficient,  1.2% user waste,  1.4 MB wasted
#   php builder        99.2% efficient,  1.1% user waste,  1.2 MB wasted
#   python runtime     99.3% efficient,  1.5% user waste,  0.6 MB wasted
#   python development 96.7% efficient,  7.1% user waste, 12.1 MB wasted
#
# All five clear .dive-ci at those numbers, which is what an earlier version of this note read as the
# condition to move them back. It is the wrong condition. A base being current is a state that flips
# every time the index serves a package ahead of the base tag, which is the exact condition the
# upgrade lines exist to answer, and the monthly rebuild leaves four times longer to occur than the
# weekly one did. Moving these five to .dive-ci would buy nothing on a day like this one and would
# make the gate pass or fail by which day it ran. They stay here until a family stops upgrading
# inherited packages at all, which § Where the reasoning lives in the docker-images rule forbids for
# a separate reason: naming a package in apk add is what brings it inside the rebuild's refresh.
rules:
  # Unique bytes over total bytes.
  lowestEfficiency: 0.88

  highestWastedBytes: 26MB

  # Wasted space as a fraction of the bytes the build itself added.
  highestUserWastedPercent: 0.30
